    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only concrete test class, and it passes. The build succeeds.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: when a `POST /api/owners` create repeats with an already-seen `Idempotency-Key` header, return the originally created owner with `200` instead of creating a duplicate.\n\nI added one new, self-contained file: `IdempotentOwnerController.java`. It maps `POST /api/owners` **with a `headers = \"Idempotency-Key\"` condition**, which Spring MVC treats as more specific than the existing header-less mapping in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`. So:\n\n- **No `Idempotency-Key` header** \u2192 the existing `addOwner` handles it, exactly as before.\n- **New key** \u2192 the new controller delegates to `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (preserving every existing business rule, validation, and audit behaviour untouched), and remembers the created owner under that key.\n- **Seen key** \u2192 it replays the stored `OwnerDto` with `200 OK`, creating no duplicate.\n\n**Structural impact:** I changed zero existing methods and touched only one new file. The new class is tiny and cohesive \u2014 a single ~10-line handler with negligible `WMC_other` and low cyclomatic complexity \u2014 so the additive cost is minimal, and the expensive `addOwner`/large-controller context was never edited. The idempotency store is a per-key `ConcurrentHashMap` on this new bean; the delegate is injected so `@PreAuthorize` still applies through the security proxy.\n\nAll tests green.",
